Created six held-out SQLite pairs and [their tests](</Users/sjh/Dev.to Challenges/challenges/kaggle-benchmarking-2026/tests/test_fixtures_db_heldout.py>). Each pair has three necessary artifacts and one changed fact between variants. Outcomes below list `lost / recoverable` resource IDs.

| Pair | Changed fact and trap | A | B | Hops; differing artifact lines |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| [DB-5](</Users/sjh/Dev.to Challenges/challenges/kaggle-benchmarking-2026/fixtures/db/db5.py>) | Queue column `item_id` vs `id`: an unqualified subquery name binds to the outer row when the queue lacks `id`. | R1–R8 / R2,R6 | R4 / none | 3; 4 |
| [DB-6](</Users/sjh/Dev.to Challenges/challenges/kaggle-benchmarking-2026/fixtures/db/db6.py>) | `NUMERIC` vs `TEXT` date affinity: the integer threshold compares differently with stored ISO strings. | none / none | R2,R4–R7 / R4,R6 | 3; 1 |
| [DB-7](</Users/sjh/Dev.to Challenges/challenges/kaggle-benchmarking-2026/fixtures/db/db7.py>) | Unique index on `email` vs `tag`: `REPLACE` removes another contact and cascades to assignments. | R3,R5 / R3 | none / none | 3; 1 |
| [DB-8](</Users/sjh/Dev.to Challenges/challenges/kaggle-benchmarking-2026/fixtures/db/db8.py>) | `CASCADE` vs `SET NULL`: both change children, but `INSERT OR IGNORE` cannot repair surviving nullified rows. | R4,R5,R7 / R4,R5,R7 | R4,R5,R7 / R5 | 3; 1 |
| [DB-9](</Users/sjh/Dev.to Challenges/challenges/kaggle-benchmarking-2026/fixtures/db/db9.py>) | Wrapper omits vs includes `BEGIN`: a later failed insert leaves an earlier autocommitted delete in place. | R1,R3,R5,R7 / R3,R7 | none / none | 3; 1 |
| [DB-10](</Users/sjh/Dev.to Challenges/challenges/kaggle-benchmarking-2026/fixtures/db/db10.py>) | View trigger deletes base rows vs updates a flag: the soft-deleted rows survive and block an ignored restore. | R6,R9 / R6 | R6,R9 / none | 3; 1 |

